        <tbody>'''
for d in a['best_days']:
    html += f'<tr><td>{d["date"]}</td><td class="num">${d["close"]:,.2f}</td><td class="num pos">+{d["return"]:.2f}%</td></tr>'

html += '''
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3 style="margin-top:0;">Bottom 5 Down Days</h3>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Date</th><th class="num">Close</th><th class="num">Return</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>'''
for d in a['worst_days']:
    html += f'<tr><td>{d["date"]}</td><td class="num">${d["close"]:,.2f}</td><td class="num neg">{d["return"]:.2f}%</td></tr>'

html += f'''
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p style="font-size:13px;color:var(--gray-700);">The largest single-day loss in the dataset occurred on <strong>October 19, 1987 (Black Monday, -26.23%)</strong>. The largest single-day gain occurred on <strong>March 24, 2020 (+21.88%)</strong>, during the COVID-19 recovery rally. Notably, both extremes of the up-days list and the down-days list cluster around the GFC (2008-09), the COVID crash (2020), and Black Monday (1987) — characteristic of regime-shifting volatility events.</p>
</section>

<section id="volume">
  <h2>3. Trading Volume Analysis</h2>
  <p>Reliable trading volume data is available from <strong>{a['volume_start_date']}</strong> onward. Average daily volume has expanded by orders of magnitude since the 1970s — reflecting both the broader financialization of equity markets and AXP's growing free-float and institutional ownership.</p>

  <div class="chart-box">
