  </div>

  <h3>Decade Narratives</h3>
  <p><strong>1970s ({a['decades'][0]['cagr']:+.1f}% CAGR).</strong> A challenging entry decade for AXP shareholders. The split-adjusted price fell from ${a['decades'][0]['start_price']:.2f} to ${a['decades'][0]['end_price']:.2f} amid the 1973-74 oil shock, stagflation, and a 74.7% peak-to-trough drawdown.</p>
  <p><strong>1980s ({a['decades'][1]['cagr']:+.1f}% CAGR).</strong> Post-Volcker disinflation and financial-sector deregulation drove a +{a['decades'][1]['total_return']:.0f}% total return. The decade also encompassed Black Monday (Oct 19, 1987, -26.23% in one session).</p>
  <p><strong>1990s ({a['decades'][2]['cagr']:+.1f}% CAGR).</strong> The strongest decade in the dataset by total return: +{a['decades'][2]['total_return']:.0f}%. AXP benefited from the long economic expansion and the run-up in financial equities. The 1994 spin-off of Lehman Brothers reshaped the corporate structure.</p>
  <p><strong>2000s ({a['decades'][3]['cagr']:+.2f}% CAGR).</strong> The "lost decade" — essentially flat ({a['decades'][3]['total_return']:+.1f}%) net of two major bear markets and the steepest drawdown in AXP's history (-83.9% during the GFC). Annualized volatility peaked at {a['decades'][3]['volatility']:.1f}%.</p>
  <p><strong>2010s ({a['decades'][4]['cagr']:+.1f}% CAGR).</strong> Recovery and re-rating: +{a['decades'][4]['total_return']:.0f}% total return at the lowest decadal volatility ({a['decades'][4]['volatility']:.1f}%). Dividend distributions accelerated to ${a['decades'][4]['dividends']:.2f} per share.</p>
