</section>

<section id="price-trend">
  <h2>1. Long-Term Price Trend Analysis</h2>
  <p>The chart below traces AXP's monthly closing price across more than five decades. The split- and dividend-adjusted series reveals the underlying compounding power of the franchise, while logarithmic scaling makes the early decades — when the adjusted price traded below $5 — visible alongside the modern-era prices in the hundreds.</p>

  <div class="chart-box tall">
    <div class="chart-title">Monthly Closing Price (Split/Dividend-Adjusted, Log Scale) — June 1972 to March 2026</div>
    <div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="priceChart"></canvas></div>
  </div>

  <h3>All-Time Extremes</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Date</th><th class="num">Close Price</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>All-Time High</strong></td><td>{a['extremes']['ath_date']}</td><td class="num pos">${a['extremes']['ath_price']:,.2f}</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>All-Time Low</strong></td><td>{a['extremes']['atl_date']}</td><td class="num neg">${a['extremes']['atl_price']:.4f}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>First Close (Dataset Start)</td><td>{a['period']['start']}</td><td class="num">${a['kpis']['first_close']:.4f}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Last Close (Dataset End)</td><td>{a['period']['end']}</td><td class="num">${a['kpis']['last_close']:,.2f}</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Compounded Annual Growth Rate (CAGR) Across Sub-Periods</h3>
  <p>Sub-period CAGRs unmask the cyclical character of AXP's compounding. The 1990s and the post-2020 period delivered double-digit annualized growth, while the 1970s and the 2000s "lost decade" delivered essentially zero or negative annualized appreciation.</p>'''

# Compute sub-period CAGRs from data
sub_periods = [
    ('1972 → 1980', 1972, 1980),
    ('1980 → 1990', 1980, 1990),
    ('1990 → 2000', 1990, 2000),
    ('2000 → 2010', 2000, 2010),
    ('2010 → 2020', 2010, 2020),
    ('2020 → 2026', 2020, 2026),
    ('1972 → 2026 (Full Period)', 1972, 2026),
]
yr_map = {y['year']: y for y in a['yearly_returns']}
# Need first/last close per year — use yearly_returns highs as proxy. Recompute from monthly:
year_first_close = {}
year_last_close = {}
for m in a['monthly_prices']:
    yr = int(m['date'][:4])
    if yr not in year_first_close:
        year_first_close[yr] = m['close']
    year_last_close[yr] = m['close']

cagr_rows = ''
for label, y1, y2 in sub_periods:
    if y1 in year_first_close and y2 in year_last_close:
        p1 = year_first_close[y1]
        p2 = year_last_close[y2]
        yrs = y2 - y1 if y2 != y1 else 1
        cagr_v = ((p2/p1) ** (1/yrs) - 1) * 100
        tot = (p2/p1 - 1) * 100
        cls = 'pos' if cagr_v > 0 else 'neg'
        cagr_rows += f'<tr><td>{label}</td><td class="num">${p1:.2f}</td><td class="num">${p2:,.2f}</td><td class="num {cls}">{cagr_v:+.2f}%</td><td class="num {cls}">{tot:+,.1f}%</td></tr>'

html += f'''
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Period</th><th class="num">Start Price</th><th class="num">End Price</th><th class="num">CAGR</th><th class="num">Total Return</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{cagr_rows}</tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Annual Total Returns</h3>
  <p>Year-over-year price returns illustrate the dispersion of single-year outcomes. The best year was 2009 (+117.0%, the post-GFC rebound); the worst was 2008 (-63.2%, the GFC collapse).</p>
  <div class="chart-box">
    <div class="chart-title">Annual Price Returns (%) — 1972 to 2026</div>
    <div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="yearlyReturnChart"></canvas></div>
  </div>

  <h3>Identified Bull and Bear Phases</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Period</th><th class="num">Approx. Move</th><th>Context</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
